course: world
lesson: 80
title: Farms and where our food comes from
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Almost all our food comes from farms: crops grown in fields and greenhouses, and animals kept for milk, eggs, meat and wool; fish come from the sea or from fish farms.
explain: Most food is changed before we buy it: wheat is ground into flour and baked into bread, milk is made into cheese and butter, and sugar comes from sugar cane or sugar beet.
explain: Farmers plough the soil, sow seeds, water and feed the crops, protect them from pests and harvest them when they are ripe; changing the crop in a field each year keeps the soil healthy.
explain: Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, such as apples, strawberries and almonds, so without them we would have much less fruit.
explain: A bowl of breakfast can come from all over the world: oats from a cold, wet field, bananas from a warm tropical farm, and milk from a cow on a farm close by.
fact: Bread is made from flour, and flour is ground from wheat grains.
fact: Butter and cheese are made from milk; a farm that keeps cows for their milk is called a dairy farm.
fact: Sugar comes from sugar cane or sugar beet.
fact: Rice, wheat and maize are the most important crops in the world.
fact: Rice grows best in warm, wet fields that are often flooded.
fact: Bananas, cocoa and coffee grow only in warm tropical places.
fact: Cows, goats and sheep give milk.
fact: Wool comes from sheep.
fact: Chocolate is made from the seeds of the cocoa tree.
fact: Bees pollinate many fruit crops, such as apples and strawberries.
fact: Growing a different crop in a field each year keeps the soil healthy.
fact: A greenhouse keeps plants warm so crops can grow in cold seasons.
exercise: What is bread mostly made from? | Flour ground from wheat | Milk from cows | Sugar from beets | Oil from olives
exercise: Which food is made from milk? | Cheese | Bread | Honey | Rice
exercise: Where does sugar come from? | Sugar cane or sugar beet | Salt water from the sea | Wheat and barley grains | The milk of goats
exercise: Which three crops feed most people in the world? | Rice, wheat and maize | Apples, pears and plums | Tea, coffee and cocoa | Carrots, onions and peas
exercise: Where do bananas grow? | In warm tropical places | In cold northern forests | In dry deserts without water | High on snowy mountains
exercise: What is chocolate made from? | The seeds of the cocoa tree | The roots of the sugar beet | The milk of brown cows | The beans of the coffee plant
exercise: Which animal gives us wool? | The sheep | The pig | The hen | The cow
exercise: Why are bees important to farmers? | They pollinate many fruit crops | They eat the pests in the soil | They plough the fields with their legs | They make the rain fall on crops
exercise: Why does a farmer grow a different crop in the field each year? | To keep the soil healthy | To confuse the birds | Because seeds cannot be reused | Because the law says so
exercise: What does a greenhouse do? | Keeps plants warm in cold seasons | Keeps plants in total darkness all day | Keeps wild animals away from the farm | Turns the rain into useful fertiliser
exercise: What does a farmer do at harvest time? | Gathers the ripe crops | Sows the new seeds | Ploughs the empty field | Waters the young shoots
exercise: Where does rice grow best? | In warm, wet, often flooded fields | In dry sandy fields with no water at all | On the cold rocky tops of high mountains | Under the snow during the long winter
exercise: Which animal gives us both milk and wool? | The sheep | The hen | The pig | The horse
exercise: Where do fish for food come from? | The sea, rivers and fish farms | Only from the freezers in shops | Fields of wheat and maize | Trees in tropical forests
test: A cheese sandwich is on your plate. Which farms helped to make it? | A wheat farm and a dairy farm | A sugar farm and a fish farm | A cocoa farm and a wool farm | A banana farm and a rice farm
test: Why do people in Norway buy bananas from other countries? | Bananas need a warm tropical climate | Norway has no shops for fruit | Bananas grow only in the sea near warm beaches | Norwegian bananas are too big
test: What would happen to apple farms if all the bees disappeared? | There would be far fewer apples | Apples would grow bigger | Nothing, apples need no flowers | Apples would grow all year
test: Which is true? | Butter is made from milk | Butter is made from wheat | Butter grows on a butter tree | Butter comes from sugar beet
test: A farmer grows wheat in the same field year after year and the harvest gets smaller. What could help? | Growing a different crop in turn | Planting the wheat closer together | Watering only at night | Ploughing the field twice a day
test: How can a farmer in a cold country grow tomatoes in early spring? | In a heated greenhouse | In a deep, dark cellar | In a snowy field | In a flooded rice field
test: A chocolate bar has cocoa, sugar and milk. Which of these grows only in the tropics? | Cocoa | Sugar beet | Milk | All of them grow anywhere
test: Which food has been changed the most from what came off the farm? | Bread | A fresh apple | A raw carrot | A boiled egg
test: Why do many countries grow lots of rice, wheat or maize? | These crops feed the most people | They need no water at all | They grow only in very cold places | They grow without any soil
test: where's chocolate even come from | The seeds of the cocoa tree | The beans of the coffee plant | The roots of sugar beet | The milk of cows mixed with sugar
test: why do farmers grow different stuff in the same field each year | It keeps the soil healthy | It confuses the birds that eat crops | It makes the field easier to plough | It stops the rain from washing seeds away
test: Leo says bananas could grow fine in a cold field in Canada. true? | No, bananas grow only in warm tropical places | Yes, bananas grow anywhere with enough rain | Yes, but only in the summer months | No, bananas only grow in deserts
test: my brother reckons bread's made from rice. right? | No, bread is made from flour ground from wheat | Yes, most bread is made from rice | Yes, rice flour is the only kind used | No, bread is made from maize only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of flour? | It is ground from wheat grains | It is pressed from sugar beet | It is made from the milk of cows | It is extracted from the seeds of cocoa
exam: Identify the material from which chocolate is manufactured. | The seeds of the cocoa tree | The leaves of the coffee plant | The roots of the sugar beet | The fruit of the banana plant
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cultivation of different crops in a field in successive years. | It keeps the soil healthy | It exhausts the soil completely | It is prohibited in most countries | It prevents bees from pollinating
exam: Ascertain the conditions under which rice grows best. | Warm, wet fields that are often flooded | Cold, dry fields upon high mountains | Sandy deserts with little water | Frozen ground beneath the snow
